▸ Context recall

This call chooses the context the turn runs on: what to keep of the turns in
hand, plus what to recall from memory. The turn then runs on the two together,
in time order.

Current user input:
{{ user_input }}

{{ recall_reply }}

Reply with ONE JSON object holding the fields above, and nothing else. An empty
object means the turns in hand are enough for this turn.

The two fields are decided independently, which makes six cases. One example
each:

1. Keep the turns in hand, recall nothing — this turn reads on its own.

  input 把这段话翻成英文
    → {}

2. Keep part of them, recall nothing — the plan is in hand, the tool output
around it is not.

  input 就按刚才那个方案来吧
    → {"context": [31, 33]}

3. Keep none of them, recall nothing.

  input 忘掉前面那些
    → {"context": "clear"}

4. Keep them and recall more — a period, and no topic.

  input 这两天都做了些什么？
    → {"recall": {"since": "yesterday"}}

5. Keep part and recall more — the turn that answered this has left the
context, and the tool dumps around it are dead weight.

  input 刚才问首经贸管工学院院长是谁？
    → {"context": [27, 28], "recall": {"query": "首经贸 管理工程学院 院长"}}

6. Keep none and recall — a topic within a period.

  input 上周关于校庆都说了什么？
    → {"context": "clear", "recall": {"query": "首经贸 校庆", "since": "last week"}}

A query is matched against stored turns — their user messages, the tools they
called, their answers. Name what the turn needs, in the words a stored turn
would contain. A follow-up's subject is in the conversation above, so carry it
into the query:

  input 那人工智能学院呢？ — the subject came three turns earlier
    → {"recall": {"query": "首经贸 人工智能学院 成立"}}

The turn ids in a keep-list are the ones in the [INFO: …] footnote of the
message that opens each turn — every turn in the context carries one. A
keep-list names what to keep; an id that is not in the context names nothing.
